1
00:00:01,059 --> 00:00:04,553
On day 151, there is frost on everything.

2
00:00:05,087 --> 00:00:06,399
On the gantry rail.

3
00:00:06,399 --> 00:00:07,710
On the canopy roof.

4
00:00:07,710 --> 00:00:09,635
On the windows of the coach,

5
00:00:09,635 --> 00:00:11,255
where eleven faces stare out

6
00:00:11,255 --> 00:00:13,916
through a thin white fur of ice.

7
00:00:13,916 --> 00:00:15,252
Down in the bunker,

8
00:00:15,252 --> 00:00:18,763
the four of you sit with your hands wrapped around nothing,

9
00:00:18,763 --> 00:00:19,581
because there is

10
00:00:19,581 --> 00:00:21,653
nothing warm to wrap them around.

11
00:00:21,653 --> 00:00:23,288
Your name is Patch.

12
00:00:23,288 --> 00:00:24,788
A hundred and fifty days ago,

13
00:00:24,788 --> 00:00:27,556
you were waiting for a bus.

14
00:00:27,556 --> 00:00:30,158
Since then, you have lived under a

15
00:00:30,158 --> 00:00:32,993
bus depot with Nora, who used to clean it,

16
00:00:32,993 --> 00:00:35,425
and Wes, who used to live on a roof.

17
00:00:35,425 --> 00:00:37,467
Last week, the woman from the truck

18
00:00:37,467 --> 00:00:39,554
crossed the street on a rope above a few

19
00:00:39,554 --> 00:00:42,910
thousand of the dead, and now she lives here too.

20
00:00:42,910 --> 00:00:44,120
The yard is full.

21
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:45,804
The street is full.

22
00:00:45,804 --> 00:00:47,382
And yesterday, she looked at the

23
00:00:47,382 --> 00:00:49,777
coach in the middle of it all and said the

24
00:00:49,777 --> 00:00:52,170
only sentence that matters this winter.

25
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:53,509
"If that coach could drive,

26
00:00:53,509 --> 00:00:54,852
I could take your whole yard

27
00:00:54,852 --> 00:00:56,273
out of that gate behind it."

28
00:00:56,273 --> 00:00:57,813
She counts on her mitten

29
00:00:57,813 --> 00:00:59,715
fingers what a bus needs.

30
00:01:00,025 --> 00:01:00,566
"Keys.

31
00:01:00,566 --> 00:01:01,558
A battery.

32
00:01:01,558 --> 00:01:04,043
And an empty driver's seat."

33
00:01:04,043 --> 00:01:06,956
The keys are in the pocket of a dead man.

34
00:01:07,267 --> 00:01:09,626
The battery has been dead since day three.

35
00:01:09,626 --> 00:01:12,101
And the driver's seat is surrounded

36
00:01:12,101 --> 00:01:13,645
by eleven passengers who have been

37
00:01:13,645 --> 00:01:17,619
waiting for this bus to leave since day 55.

38
00:01:17,619 --> 00:01:19,690
"So," says Wes.

39
00:01:19,690 --> 00:01:20,391
"Easy."

40
00:01:20,391 --> 00:01:23,521
The woman explains the rest the

41
00:01:23,521 --> 00:01:25,794
way other people explain a recipe.

42
00:01:25,794 --> 00:01:27,444
Somebody opens the gate.

43
00:01:27,444 --> 00:01:28,973
She drives the coach out,

44
00:01:28,973 --> 00:01:31,047
slowly, with the horn going.

45
00:01:31,565 --> 00:01:33,225
The yard follows the engine.

46
00:01:33,225 --> 00:01:35,186
The street follows the yard.

47
00:01:35,512 --> 00:01:37,753
She leads them all the way out of town,

48
00:01:37,753 --> 00:01:39,845
over the flyover and far beyond it,

49
00:01:39,845 --> 00:01:42,951
to the last sound they will ever hear from her.

50
00:01:43,436 --> 00:01:45,014
Then she turns the engine off,

51
00:01:45,014 --> 00:01:46,156
and rolls away.

52
00:01:46,657 --> 00:01:51,685
Days 152 to 154 are

53
00:01:51,685 --> 00:01:52,953
for watching the cold.

54
00:01:52,953 --> 00:01:54,277
You have never seen the

55
00:01:54,277 --> 00:01:56,373
dead in a real frost before.

56
00:01:56,373 --> 00:01:57,306
In the mornings,

57
00:01:57,306 --> 00:01:59,792
when the yard is white, they hardly move.

58
00:01:59,792 --> 00:02:01,776
Their heads turn slowly,

59
00:02:01,776 --> 00:02:03,337
like rusty hinges.

60
00:02:03,337 --> 00:02:06,991
A shoulder takes a whole minute to shift.

61
00:02:06,991 --> 00:02:10,276
By midday, when the sun touches the concrete,

62
00:02:10,276 --> 00:02:11,548
they loosen up again,

63
00:02:11,548 --> 00:02:13,756
and the slow shuffle comes back.

64
00:02:14,852 --> 00:02:17,525
"Cold kills batteries," says the woman.

65
00:02:17,525 --> 00:02:18,561
"Apparently,

66
00:02:18,561 --> 00:02:20,756
it slows down everything else, too."

67
00:02:21,596 --> 00:02:23,008
You write it on the wall,

68
00:02:23,008 --> 00:02:24,080
next to the chart.

69
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:25,171
Not in words.

70
00:02:25,690 --> 00:02:26,958
Just a small drawing of a

71
00:02:26,958 --> 00:02:28,623
sun with a line through it.

72
00:02:29,078 --> 00:02:30,495
On mornings like that,

73
00:02:30,495 --> 00:02:31,912
you will have an hour.

74
00:02:31,912 --> 00:02:32,751
Maybe two.

75
00:02:33,879 --> 00:02:35,920
Nora watches the coach the whole time.

76
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:38,599
The man in the blue depot vest stands at

77
00:02:38,599 --> 00:02:40,833
the top of the aisle, as he always does.

78
00:02:40,833 --> 00:02:43,170
In the frost, he doesn't turn at all.

79
00:02:43,170 --> 00:02:44,302
Out in the street,

80
00:02:44,302 --> 00:02:47,397
the Doorman freezes with both hands flat on the gate,

81
00:02:47,397 --> 00:02:48,407
like a man who has

82
00:02:48,407 --> 00:02:51,800
fallen asleep waiting to be buzzed in.

83
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:54,311
On day 155, Wes climbs onto the

84
00:02:54,311 --> 00:02:56,293
canopy roof with the screwdriver.

85
00:02:56,293 --> 00:02:58,764
The roof is made of long metal sheets,

86
00:02:58,764 --> 00:03:00,310
screwed down onto beams.

87
00:03:00,747 --> 00:03:02,373
The sheet right above the front of

88
00:03:02,373 --> 00:03:04,356
the coach is held by twelve screws.

89
00:03:04,356 --> 00:03:05,912
They have been rusting there for

90
00:03:05,912 --> 00:03:08,409
longer than Nora has been cleaning the depot.

91
00:03:08,409 --> 00:03:11,733
So Wes does what he did to the bay door.

92
00:03:11,733 --> 00:03:13,119
One screw at a time.

93
00:03:13,119 --> 00:03:16,672
He waits for the wind to blow, then turns.

94
00:03:16,672 --> 00:03:19,168
When the wind stops, he stops.

95
00:03:19,590 --> 00:03:21,103
Each screw comes out with

96
00:03:21,103 --> 00:03:23,033
its own tiny, rusty squeak.

97
00:03:23,438 --> 00:03:24,140
Below him,

98
00:03:24,140 --> 00:03:27,258
the coach passengers tilt their faces up,

99
00:03:27,258 --> 00:03:29,141
slowly, and wait for the next one.

100
00:03:29,141 --> 00:03:31,287
The woman watches from the gantry.

101
00:03:31,287 --> 00:03:32,968
She doesn't like roofs.

102
00:03:32,968 --> 00:03:35,187
Roofs have one way down.

103
00:03:35,187 --> 00:03:36,790
It takes three days.

104
00:03:36,790 --> 00:03:39,839
On day 157, Wes lifts the sheet,

105
00:03:39,839 --> 00:03:42,620
and there is a hole in the canopy roof.

106
00:03:42,620 --> 00:03:43,848
You lie on your stomach

107
00:03:43,848 --> 00:03:45,479
and look down through it.

108
00:03:45,854 --> 00:03:48,342
Two meters below is the roof of the coach.

109
00:03:48,342 --> 00:03:50,072
In the middle of it,

110
00:03:50,072 --> 00:03:53,111
near the front, there is a square hatch with a handle.

111
00:03:53,470 --> 00:03:54,658
All around the coach,

112
00:03:54,658 --> 00:03:57,720
packed shoulder to shoulder, there are heads.

113
00:03:57,720 --> 00:03:59,284
"Management will want to

114
00:03:59,284 --> 00:04:02,351
know who did this," Wes whispers.

115
00:04:02,351 --> 00:04:05,031
"Management did," says Nora.

116
00:04:05,694 --> 00:04:06,627
On day 158,

117
00:04:06,627 --> 00:04:09,415
Nora tells you about the hatch.

118
00:04:09,854 --> 00:04:11,572
It is an emergency hatch.

119
00:04:11,572 --> 00:04:13,026
It opens from outside

120
00:04:13,026 --> 00:04:14,798
with a twist of the handle.

121
00:04:14,798 --> 00:04:18,043
On hot nights, at the end of the night route,

122
00:04:18,043 --> 00:04:19,294
she used to climb onto

123
00:04:19,294 --> 00:04:21,361
the coach roof from the canopy ladder

124
00:04:21,361 --> 00:04:23,335
and open it to let the air through.

125
00:04:23,335 --> 00:04:24,581
Then she would come down

126
00:04:24,581 --> 00:04:26,675
and wait at the folding door.

127
00:04:26,675 --> 00:04:27,766
"He always came down

128
00:04:27,766 --> 00:04:29,812
the aisle last," she says.

129
00:04:29,812 --> 00:04:31,986
"After the last passenger.

130
00:04:31,986 --> 00:04:34,405
He'd stand at the top of the

131
00:04:34,405 --> 00:04:36,513
steps and drop the keys into my hand.

132
00:04:36,513 --> 00:04:38,618
And he'd say, all yours."

133
00:04:38,618 --> 00:04:41,338
She looks at the wall for a while.

134
00:04:41,808 --> 00:04:43,183
"For nine years.

135
00:04:43,183 --> 00:04:44,347
All yours.

136
00:04:44,347 --> 00:04:47,007
Every single night."

137
00:04:47,007 --> 00:04:49,251
On day 159,

138
00:04:49,251 --> 00:04:51,262
she draws the coach for you, from above.

139
00:04:51,262 --> 00:04:52,235
The hatch.

140
00:04:52,235 --> 00:04:52,944
The aisle.

141
00:04:52,944 --> 00:04:54,685
The step down to the door.

142
00:04:54,685 --> 00:04:57,081
The driver's seat, on the left.

143
00:04:57,081 --> 00:04:59,116
And a cross on the man in the vest,

144
00:04:59,116 --> 00:05:01,914
on his chest, on the right-hand pocket.

145
00:05:01,914 --> 00:05:03,739
"Right pocket," she says.

146
00:05:04,097 --> 00:05:04,918
"Always the right.

147
00:05:04,918 --> 00:05:06,724
He said the left one had a hole."

148
00:05:07,548 --> 00:05:08,399
On day 160,

149
00:05:08,399 --> 00:05:11,717
nobody mentions how much food is left.

150
00:05:12,075 --> 00:05:13,889
Nobody has to.

151
00:05:13,889 --> 00:05:16,960
From day 161 to day 163,

152
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:20,281
you get ready, and you get hungry.

153
00:05:20,281 --> 00:05:22,013
Nora lays everything out on

154
00:05:22,013 --> 00:05:24,337
the bench and does the arithmetic.

155
00:05:24,337 --> 00:05:26,454
Two weeks, if you are careful.

156
00:05:26,812 --> 00:05:28,475
The tins are almost gone.

157
00:05:28,475 --> 00:05:29,946
The crackers are gone.

158
00:05:29,946 --> 00:05:32,362
The bottom of the box of sugared almonds

159
00:05:32,362 --> 00:05:35,460
is visible for the first time since day 59.

160
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:38,583
"The wedding's nearly over," says Wes.

161
00:05:38,583 --> 00:05:40,002
On the hill's radio,

162
00:05:40,002 --> 00:05:43,891
the young voice says they have food up there.

163
00:05:43,891 --> 00:05:44,653
Potatoes.

164
00:05:44,653 --> 00:05:45,840
Apples.

165
00:05:45,840 --> 00:05:47,019
Jars of beans.

166
00:05:47,019 --> 00:05:49,848
They just have no truck to bring it,

167
00:05:49,848 --> 00:05:52,275
and no street to bring it through.

168
00:05:52,275 --> 00:05:53,326
For once,

169
00:05:53,326 --> 00:05:56,601
it's the hill that can't reach anybody.

170
00:05:56,601 --> 00:05:59,156
On day 162, you notice that the

171
00:05:59,156 --> 00:06:01,055
woman eats half of what she is given

172
00:06:01,055 --> 00:06:03,381
and slides the rest back into the

173
00:06:03,381 --> 00:06:05,548
box when she thinks nobody is looking.

174
00:06:05,548 --> 00:06:06,848
Nora notices too.

175
00:06:07,223 --> 00:06:09,450
The next day, Nora hands her an extra

176
00:06:09,450 --> 00:06:12,105
cracker and stares at her until she eats it.

177
00:06:12,105 --> 00:06:14,237
That is the second peace treaty.

178
00:06:14,852 --> 00:06:16,064
Wes builds the hook.

179
00:06:16,064 --> 00:06:18,026
He takes the long handle from the

180
00:06:18,026 --> 00:06:20,416
old floor polisher you pulled off the

181
00:06:20,416 --> 00:06:22,216
maintenance stairs on day 19,

182
00:06:22,216 --> 00:06:25,333
and tapes a bent piece of wire to the end of it.

183
00:06:25,333 --> 00:06:28,129
He tests it on the woman's scarf.

184
00:06:28,129 --> 00:06:29,681
It catches perfectly.

185
00:06:29,681 --> 00:06:31,904
She lets him know, with one look,

186
00:06:31,904 --> 00:06:35,471
that he will never test it on her scarf again.

187
00:06:35,471 --> 00:06:36,901
The plan is simple,

188
00:06:36,901 --> 00:06:39,641
the way the worst plans always are.

189
00:06:39,641 --> 00:06:41,304
On the first hard frost,

190
00:06:41,304 --> 00:06:44,180
you go down through the hole onto the coach roof.

191
00:06:44,180 --> 00:06:45,828
Nora comes with you.

192
00:06:45,828 --> 00:06:47,396
She says he turns for her.

193
00:06:47,396 --> 00:06:49,612
Wes and the woman hold the rope.

194
00:06:50,179 --> 00:06:54,194
On the night of day 163, the sky clears.

195
00:06:54,194 --> 00:06:56,379
The stars come out, sharp and close.

196
00:06:56,379 --> 00:06:59,149
Nora looks up at them from the gantry.

197
00:06:59,149 --> 00:07:01,046
"Tomorrow," she says.

198
00:07:01,982 --> 00:07:03,985
At dawn on day 164,

199
00:07:03,985 --> 00:07:07,690
the yard is white from the gate to the canopy.

200
00:07:07,690 --> 00:07:08,928
The dead stand in it

201
00:07:08,928 --> 00:07:10,913
like statues in a garden.

202
00:07:10,913 --> 00:07:12,686
Frost on their shoulders.

203
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:14,582
Frost on their heads.

204
00:07:14,582 --> 00:07:16,752
Their breath doesn't show,

205
00:07:16,752 --> 00:07:18,629
because they don't have any.

206
00:07:18,629 --> 00:07:20,925
You climb over the gantry rail

207
00:07:20,925 --> 00:07:23,134
with the rope around your waist and

208
00:07:23,134 --> 00:07:25,485
the polisher handle in your hand.

209
00:07:25,485 --> 00:07:27,200
Across the canopy roof

210
00:07:27,200 --> 00:07:29,329
on your hands and knees.

211
00:07:29,329 --> 00:07:31,065
Each step booms.

212
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:33,078
Below, a few thousand heads

213
00:07:33,078 --> 00:07:35,159
begin to turn toward the sound.

214
00:07:35,597 --> 00:07:37,542
Very, very slowly.

215
00:07:37,542 --> 00:07:40,757
At the hole, Wes and the woman

216
00:07:40,757 --> 00:07:42,849
brace their feet and lower you down.

217
00:07:42,849 --> 00:07:45,284
Your feet touch the roof of the coach.

218
00:07:45,284 --> 00:07:46,625
It is so cold that it

219
00:07:46,625 --> 00:07:48,649
burns through your shoes.

220
00:07:48,649 --> 00:07:49,598
Under the frost,

221
00:07:49,598 --> 00:07:52,146
it gives a little, like thin ice.

222
00:07:52,146 --> 00:07:53,906
The crowd is right there,

223
00:07:53,906 --> 00:07:57,830
on every side, an arm's length below the edge of the roof.

224
00:07:58,236 --> 00:07:59,553
Faces tilt up.

225
00:07:59,553 --> 00:08:00,869
Eyes find you.

226
00:08:00,869 --> 00:08:02,897
Nothing else moves.

227
00:08:02,897 --> 00:08:05,221
Then Nora comes down the rope behind you,

228
00:08:05,221 --> 00:08:07,270
the purple band on her arm,

229
00:08:07,270 --> 00:08:09,762
the flashlight on its cord around her wrist.

230
00:08:09,762 --> 00:08:12,145
For a woman who hated every rung of

231
00:08:12,145 --> 00:08:14,968
the canopy ladder, she lands very gently.

232
00:08:14,968 --> 00:08:16,662
She looks down once at the

233
00:08:16,662 --> 00:08:18,489
crowd around the coach,

234
00:08:18,489 --> 00:08:21,125
closes her eyes, and opens them again.

235
00:08:21,125 --> 00:08:22,686
Then she nods at you.

236
00:08:22,686 --> 00:08:24,242
You crawl to the hatch.

237
00:08:24,242 --> 00:08:26,199
The handle is frozen.

238
00:08:26,199 --> 00:08:27,120
You twist.

239
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:27,874
Nothing.

240
00:08:27,874 --> 00:08:30,336
You twist again, harder.

241
00:08:30,336 --> 00:08:32,959
With a crack like a snapped stick,

242
00:08:32,959 --> 00:08:34,812
it turns, and the hatch lifts.

243
00:08:34,812 --> 00:08:37,760
The smell of the coach comes up to meet you:

244
00:08:37,760 --> 00:08:38,491
old seats,

245
00:08:38,491 --> 00:08:42,068
cold air, a hundred and nine days of closed windows.

246
00:08:42,068 --> 00:08:44,851
You look down into the aisle.

247
00:08:44,851 --> 00:08:46,834
Eleven faces look back up at you.

248
00:08:46,834 --> 00:08:49,097
And at the very top of the aisle,

249
00:08:49,097 --> 00:08:51,744
right below the hatch, in a blue depot vest

250
00:08:51,744 --> 00:08:54,352
with two pockets, stands the night driver.

251
00:08:54,352 --> 00:08:55,971
He lifts his head

252
00:08:55,971 --> 00:08:57,548
toward the light, slowly.

253
00:08:57,548 --> 00:08:59,304
His arms start to rise.

254
00:08:59,743 --> 00:09:01,086
You lower the polisher

255
00:09:01,086 --> 00:09:02,604
handle through the hatch.

256
00:09:02,604 --> 00:09:03,957
Down past his face.

257
00:09:03,957 --> 00:09:06,151
Down past his reaching hands.

258
00:09:06,151 --> 00:09:07,558
Toward his chest.

259
00:09:07,558 --> 00:09:09,732
Toward the right-hand pocket.

260
00:09:09,732 --> 00:09:11,002
Behind him,

261
00:09:11,002 --> 00:09:15,744
ten passengers have started up the aisle, one slow step at a time.

262
00:09:15,744 --> 00:09:17,570
The wire touches the pocket.

263
00:09:17,570 --> 00:09:18,562
Slides in.

264
00:09:18,953 --> 00:09:20,763
You feel it catch on something.

265
00:09:20,763 --> 00:09:21,629
You pull.

266
00:09:22,084 --> 00:09:24,138
His hand closes around the handle.

267
00:09:24,138 --> 00:09:25,435
You pull harder.

268
00:09:25,858 --> 00:09:26,921
He pulls back.

269
00:09:26,921 --> 00:09:29,615
For a moment, you are in a tug of war

270
00:09:29,615 --> 00:09:32,777
with a man who has been dead since the first week,

271
00:09:32,777 --> 00:09:35,108
over the keys to his own bus.

272
00:09:35,108 --> 00:09:36,537
And he is winning.

273
00:09:36,537 --> 00:09:39,660
Nora leans over the hatch beside you.

274
00:09:39,660 --> 00:09:40,950
She does not shout.

275
00:09:40,950 --> 00:09:43,347
Nothing here is allowed to be loud.

276
00:09:43,347 --> 00:09:46,396
She just holds out her open mitten hand,

277
00:09:46,396 --> 00:09:48,168
the way she did at the bottom of

278
00:09:48,168 --> 00:09:50,708
the coach steps every night for nine years,

279
00:09:50,708 --> 00:09:53,826
and says one word, very softly.

280
00:09:53,826 --> 00:09:54,711
"Keys."

281
00:09:55,487 --> 00:09:56,387
The night driver

282
00:09:56,387 --> 00:09:57,817
turns his face toward her.

283
00:09:57,817 --> 00:09:59,647
His hand lets go of the

284
00:09:59,647 --> 00:10:02,087
handle and reaches for her instead.

285
00:10:02,087 --> 00:10:04,125
Up, up toward the hatch.

286
00:10:04,125 --> 00:10:05,304
And while he reaches,

287
00:10:05,304 --> 00:10:08,243
you pull the polisher handle out through the hatch,

288
00:10:08,243 --> 00:10:10,050
and on the end of the wire,

289
00:10:10,050 --> 00:10:12,493
swinging, there is a ring of keys.

290
00:10:12,493 --> 00:10:14,382
Nora closes the hatch.

291
00:10:14,819 --> 00:10:15,631
Very gently.

292
00:10:15,631 --> 00:10:18,244
The handle turns with a small click.

293
00:10:19,066 --> 00:10:20,954
"All yours," she whispers.

294
00:10:20,954 --> 00:10:24,068
Then the sun comes over the rooftops.

295
00:10:24,068 --> 00:10:25,842
You feel it before you see it.

296
00:10:25,842 --> 00:10:28,562
A little warmth on the back of your neck.

297
00:10:28,562 --> 00:10:29,866
And all around the coach,

298
00:10:29,866 --> 00:10:31,785
the frost on a few thousand

299
00:10:31,785 --> 00:10:33,578
shoulders starts to turn to water.

300
00:10:33,578 --> 00:10:34,602
The heads that turned

301
00:10:34,602 --> 00:10:36,697
slowly start to turn faster.

302
00:10:36,697 --> 00:10:37,876
A hand comes up over

303
00:10:37,876 --> 00:10:39,977
the edge of the coach roof.

304
00:10:39,977 --> 00:10:40,797
Then another.

305
00:10:40,797 --> 00:10:42,143
The coach rocks on its

306
00:10:42,143 --> 00:10:44,761
springs as the crowd leans in.

307
00:10:44,761 --> 00:10:46,797
"Rope," you whisper.

308
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:48,578
Nora goes first.

309
00:10:48,578 --> 00:10:51,732
Wes and the woman pull her up through

310
00:10:51,732 --> 00:10:53,630
the hole like a bucket out of a well.

311
00:10:53,630 --> 00:10:55,313
Then the rope comes down again,

312
00:10:55,313 --> 00:10:57,959
and you grab it, and the keys are in your teeth,

313
00:10:57,959 --> 00:11:00,262
because you need both hands.

314
00:11:00,262 --> 00:11:01,806
The coach rocks again.

315
00:11:01,806 --> 00:11:04,004
Green fingers brush your heel.

316
00:11:04,004 --> 00:11:06,129
Then you are through the hole,

317
00:11:06,129 --> 00:11:08,154
lying on the canopy roof,

318
00:11:08,154 --> 00:11:11,677
looking at the sky, with a ring of keys in your mouth.

319
00:11:11,677 --> 00:11:14,359
You do not let go of it for a long time.

320
00:11:14,893 --> 00:11:20,364
From day 165 to day 169,

321
00:11:20,364 --> 00:11:22,343
the keys hang on a nail in the bunker,

322
00:11:22,343 --> 00:11:23,632
next to the chart.

323
00:11:24,054 --> 00:11:25,162
Nobody touches them.

324
00:11:25,162 --> 00:11:26,728
They just look at them.

325
00:11:27,086 --> 00:11:28,802
Five keys on a steel ring.

326
00:11:28,802 --> 00:11:30,936
One is big and black.

327
00:11:30,936 --> 00:11:32,622
One is small and silver.

328
00:11:32,622 --> 00:11:33,769
One has a little

329
00:11:33,769 --> 00:11:35,855
plastic tag shaped like a bus.

330
00:11:35,855 --> 00:11:38,377
Nora sits on the bench in the evenings

331
00:11:38,377 --> 00:11:40,665
and watches them in the lantern light.

332
00:11:40,665 --> 00:11:42,353
She doesn't say anything.

333
00:11:42,984 --> 00:11:46,416
On day 167, she gets up,

334
00:11:46,416 --> 00:11:49,478
takes the ring off the nail, holds it in her open hand

335
00:11:49,478 --> 00:11:52,212
for a long minute, and hangs it back up.

336
00:11:52,212 --> 00:11:54,322
Then she goes to bed early.

337
00:11:54,322 --> 00:11:55,415
You don't ask.

338
00:11:55,415 --> 00:11:57,609
Some things are all hers.

339
00:11:58,143 --> 00:12:00,478
On day 168, the woman takes the

340
00:12:00,478 --> 00:12:02,714
big black key off the nail,

341
00:12:02,714 --> 00:12:05,107
weighs it in her hand, and puts it back.

342
00:12:05,754 --> 00:12:07,484
"One out of three," she says.

343
00:12:07,484 --> 00:12:09,009
"Now the battery."

344
00:12:10,009 --> 00:12:14,757
From day 170 to day 173,

345
00:12:15,147 --> 00:12:16,672
you work on the battery.

346
00:12:16,672 --> 00:12:18,844
The solar panel on the canopy roof

347
00:12:18,844 --> 00:12:21,071
feeds a battery in the control box,

348
00:12:21,071 --> 00:12:23,594
the one that runs the loudspeaker.

349
00:12:23,594 --> 00:12:26,858
The woman says a battery is a battery.

350
00:12:26,858 --> 00:12:28,701
In the maintenance cupboard,

351
00:12:28,701 --> 00:12:32,177
Nora finds two long cables with heavy clips on the ends,

352
00:12:32,177 --> 00:12:34,960
coiled up behind the yellow paint shelf,

353
00:12:34,960 --> 00:12:37,241
where nobody has touched them for years.

354
00:12:37,838 --> 00:12:39,552
"Jump leads," says the woman,

355
00:12:39,552 --> 00:12:42,351
as if somebody has given her flowers.

356
00:12:42,351 --> 00:12:43,664
But the coach's battery

357
00:12:43,664 --> 00:12:45,333
is not inside the coach.

358
00:12:45,882 --> 00:12:48,268
Nora says it sits behind a small door low

359
00:12:48,268 --> 00:12:50,853
on its side, just behind the front wheel.

360
00:12:50,853 --> 00:12:52,550
Down where the crowd is.

361
00:12:52,941 --> 00:12:57,244
So on the frost morning of day 172,

362
00:12:57,244 --> 00:12:59,272
you go back down through the hole.

363
00:12:59,272 --> 00:13:01,216
This time, Wes comes too.

364
00:13:01,216 --> 00:13:03,514
He lies flat on the coach roof and

365
00:13:03,514 --> 00:13:05,946
holds your ankles, and you hang head

366
00:13:05,946 --> 00:13:07,630
first over the side of the coach,

367
00:13:07,630 --> 00:13:08,776
a little at a time,

368
00:13:08,776 --> 00:13:12,060
until the battery door is right in front of your face.

369
00:13:12,595 --> 00:13:14,195
On the other side of the door,

370
00:13:14,195 --> 00:13:15,692
a frozen walker stands close

371
00:13:15,692 --> 00:13:17,841
enough to share a scarf with.

372
00:13:17,841 --> 00:13:19,653
Its head is turning toward you.

373
00:13:19,653 --> 00:13:20,406
Slowly.

374
00:13:20,796 --> 00:13:21,381
Slowly.

375
00:13:21,381 --> 00:13:23,581
You open the little door.

376
00:13:24,067 --> 00:13:25,715
You clip the first cable on.

377
00:13:25,715 --> 00:13:26,981
Then the second.

378
00:13:27,675 --> 00:13:29,812
The walker's face finishes turning.

379
00:13:29,812 --> 00:13:32,711
It looks at you from a hand's width away.

380
00:13:32,711 --> 00:13:34,438
Its mouth begins to open.

381
00:13:34,811 --> 00:13:35,454
Wes pulls.

382
00:13:35,454 --> 00:13:37,383
You come up over the edge of the

383
00:13:37,383 --> 00:13:39,986
roof like a sack, and the walker's hands

384
00:13:39,986 --> 00:13:41,974
close on the air where your head was.

385
00:13:42,332 --> 00:13:44,066
When you climb back through the hole,

386
00:13:44,066 --> 00:13:45,435
the woman is waiting with her

387
00:13:45,435 --> 00:13:48,342
goggles pushed up and her face very still.

388
00:13:48,342 --> 00:13:50,299
"Two out of three," she says.

389
00:13:50,299 --> 00:13:51,783
Her hands are shaking.

390
00:13:51,783 --> 00:13:53,379
You pretend not to see.

391
00:13:53,379 --> 00:13:56,382
That evening, at the other end of the cables,

392
00:13:56,382 --> 00:13:57,628
the woman clips them onto

393
00:13:57,628 --> 00:13:59,850
the battery inside the control box.

394
00:13:59,850 --> 00:14:00,843
A tiny spark.

395
00:14:00,843 --> 00:14:03,555
Nothing else happens for a while.

396
00:14:04,186 --> 00:14:05,786
Then, down in the yard,

397
00:14:05,786 --> 00:14:09,792
so faint you almost miss it, a little light comes on behind

398
00:14:09,792 --> 00:14:12,161
the coach's windshield, on the dashboard.

399
00:14:12,678 --> 00:14:14,317
"It's drinking," she says.

400
00:14:14,317 --> 00:14:16,719
"The loudspeaker will have to

401
00:14:16,719 --> 00:14:18,931
go hungry for a while," says Wes.

402
00:14:18,931 --> 00:14:21,932
Nobody in the bunker objects.

403
00:14:21,932 --> 00:14:25,877
From day 174 to day 177,

404
00:14:25,877 --> 00:14:29,044
the coach drinks sunlight.

405
00:14:29,044 --> 00:14:29,556
Every day,

406
00:14:29,556 --> 00:14:31,951
you check the little light on the dashboard.

407
00:14:32,308 --> 00:14:34,785
Every day, it glows a bit brighter.

408
00:14:34,785 --> 00:14:37,153
The sun is low and weak this time of year,

409
00:14:37,153 --> 00:14:38,703
and the panel is half covered with

410
00:14:38,703 --> 00:14:40,997
frost every morning, so you climb out

411
00:14:40,997 --> 00:14:42,454
and wipe it with your sleeve,

412
00:14:42,454 --> 00:14:45,992
one strip at a time, the way you did in the summer.

413
00:14:45,992 --> 00:14:47,662
Food is down to a week.

414
00:14:47,662 --> 00:14:50,368
Nobody counts it out loud anymore.

415
00:14:50,855 --> 00:14:52,550
The almonds are gone.

416
00:14:52,550 --> 00:14:54,168
The wedding is over.

417
00:14:54,168 --> 00:14:55,312
On day 175,

418
00:14:55,312 --> 00:14:58,249
it snows for the first time.

419
00:14:58,784 --> 00:15:01,254
Snow on the panel means no sunlight,

420
00:15:01,254 --> 00:15:03,574
so you crawl out with a broom from the

421
00:15:03,574 --> 00:15:05,685
maintenance cupboard and sweep it clean,

422
00:15:05,685 --> 00:15:08,625
while the dead below stand with white caps on

423
00:15:08,625 --> 00:15:12,289
their heads like a crowd at a winter wedding.

424
00:15:12,289 --> 00:15:13,625
On day 176,

425
00:15:14,670 --> 00:15:17,351
the hill's radio asks what the plan is.

426
00:15:17,351 --> 00:15:18,970
The woman tells them.

427
00:15:18,970 --> 00:15:21,814
There is a long silence on the other end.

428
00:15:21,814 --> 00:15:24,561
"With the passengers still on board?"

429
00:15:24,561 --> 00:15:25,895
asks the young voice.

430
00:15:25,895 --> 00:15:27,163
"We're working on

431
00:15:27,163 --> 00:15:29,535
that," says the woman.

432
00:15:29,535 --> 00:15:31,874
On the evening of day 178,

433
00:15:31,874 --> 00:15:36,143
you and Nora sit on the bench with the lantern turned low,

434
00:15:36,143 --> 00:15:38,092
the way you have on so many

435
00:15:38,092 --> 00:15:41,812
last nights before so many stupid plans.

436
00:15:42,524 --> 00:15:44,075
"If it works," you say,

437
00:15:44,075 --> 00:15:46,068
"the yard will be empty.

438
00:15:46,443 --> 00:15:48,238
The street will be empty.

439
00:15:48,238 --> 00:15:49,965
You could open a window."

440
00:15:49,965 --> 00:15:51,585
"I could," says Nora.

441
00:15:51,585 --> 00:15:52,706
"Would you leave?

442
00:15:52,706 --> 00:15:53,762
Go up the hill?"

443
00:15:53,762 --> 00:15:56,694
She thinks about it for a long time.

444
00:15:56,694 --> 00:15:57,613
"No," she says.

445
00:15:57,613 --> 00:16:00,299
"But I'd like to sit in the yard.

446
00:16:00,299 --> 00:16:01,435
In the daylight.

447
00:16:01,435 --> 00:16:02,171
Just once.

448
00:16:02,171 --> 00:16:04,061
Without anybody watching."

449
00:16:04,061 --> 00:16:05,221
On the stairs,

450
00:16:05,221 --> 00:16:07,950
Wes is pretending to be asleep.

451
00:16:07,950 --> 00:16:08,929
The woman is sitting

452
00:16:08,929 --> 00:16:11,155
against the wall, facing the door.

453
00:16:11,155 --> 00:16:13,078
Her eyes are open.

454
00:16:13,078 --> 00:16:15,304
She heard every word.

455
00:16:15,304 --> 00:16:16,615
On day 179,

456
00:16:16,615 --> 00:16:19,200
she says, "I want to see the driver's seat."

457
00:16:19,200 --> 00:16:21,968
So you take her to the hole in the canopy roof,

458
00:16:21,968 --> 00:16:23,170
and she lies on her stomach

459
00:16:23,170 --> 00:16:25,466
and looks down through it for a long time,

460
00:16:25,466 --> 00:16:26,558
past the coach roof,

461
00:16:26,558 --> 00:16:28,247
through the frosted windshield,

462
00:16:28,247 --> 00:16:31,429
at the big steering wheel and the empty seat behind it.

463
00:16:31,787 --> 00:16:33,533
Her hands move a little on the metal,

464
00:16:33,533 --> 00:16:35,763
as if they are already holding something.

465
00:16:36,121 --> 00:16:38,163
On day 180, the light on the

466
00:16:38,163 --> 00:16:41,120
coach's dashboard is bright and steady.

467
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:43,506
At dawn, the four of you stand on

468
00:16:43,506 --> 00:16:45,791
the gantry and look down at the coach.

469
00:16:45,791 --> 00:16:47,321
The battery is full.

470
00:16:47,695 --> 00:16:49,233
The keys are on the nail.

471
00:16:49,233 --> 00:16:50,594
The hole in the canopy

472
00:16:50,594 --> 00:16:52,706
roof is right above the hatch.

473
00:16:52,706 --> 00:16:53,470
And inside,

474
00:16:53,470 --> 00:16:57,364
packed between the seats, eleven passengers are waiting,

475
00:16:57,364 --> 00:16:59,789
faces turned up, as if they can

476
00:16:59,789 --> 00:17:02,209
feel that their bus is about to leave.

477
00:17:02,209 --> 00:17:03,498
"So," says Wes.

478
00:17:03,498 --> 00:17:05,730
"How do we ask them to get off?"

479
00:17:06,314 --> 00:17:07,351
Nobody answers.

480
00:17:07,351 --> 00:17:08,517
Down in the yard,

481
00:17:08,517 --> 00:17:11,613
the man in the blue depot vest stands at the

482
00:17:11,613 --> 00:17:12,476
top of the aisle,

483
00:17:12,476 --> 00:17:15,029
as he always has, right behind the driver's seat.

484
00:17:15,029 --> 00:17:16,488
Then Nora looks up at the

485
00:17:16,488 --> 00:17:19,056
loudspeaker under the solar panel.

486
00:17:19,056 --> 00:17:21,496
"We ask them politely," she says.

487
00:17:21,496 --> 00:17:23,212
A hundred and eighty days.

488
00:17:23,212 --> 00:17:26,194
The same orange bandage.

489
00:17:26,194 --> 00:17:28,525
Nora is holding the flashlight.

490
00:17:28,525 --> 00:17:31,542
You are holding the orange bag.

491
00:17:31,542 --> 00:17:33,888
Tomorrow is day 181.

492
00:17:34,439 --> 00:17:35,869
You have the keys to the

493
00:17:35,869 --> 00:17:37,414
last bus out of the depot.

494
00:17:37,414 --> 00:17:39,744
Its battery is full of winter sunlight.

495
00:17:39,744 --> 00:17:41,772
You have food for a few days.

496
00:17:42,147 --> 00:17:44,206
And between you and the driver's seat

497
00:17:44,206 --> 00:17:46,161
stand eleven passengers who have been

498
00:17:46,161 --> 00:17:49,211
waiting since day 55 for this bus to leave.

499
00:17:49,211 --> 00:17:50,503
Would you have gone

500
00:17:50,503 --> 00:17:51,865
down through the hatch?

501
00:17:51,865 --> 00:17:53,056
Which decision would

502
00:17:53,056 --> 00:17:54,689
you have made differently?

503
00:17:54,689 --> 00:17:56,183
Tell us in the comments,

504
00:17:56,183 --> 00:17:58,746
and subscribe to Last Safe Door,

505
00:17:58,746 --> 00:18:01,243
so you're here for day 181.
